lizing that. Recognition is the first step! § In some ways, the bigger news, though it hasn’t gotten as much coverage yet, is this: @axios scoop on top AI labs investigating tens of thousands of security incidents. \\nMore here: axios.com/2026/09/28/flo… \",\"username\":\"MadisonMills22\",\"name\":\"Madison Mills\",\"profile_image_url\":\"https://pbs.substack.com/profile_images/1784997467039170560/0Vttab_c_normal.jpg\",\"date\":\"2026-09-28T15:19:28.000Z\",\"photos\":[{\"img_url\":\"https://pbs.substack.com/media/HTUDGmOXEAE-5_4.jpg\",\"link_url\":\"https://t.co/RGJwUEMAsw\"}],\"quoted_tweet\":{},\"reply_count\":12,\"retweet_count\":57,\"like_count\":157,\"impression_count\":13042,\"expanded_url\":null,\"video_url\":null,\"video_preview_media_key\":null,\"belowTheFold\":true}\" data-component-name=\"Twitter2ToDOM\"> This is a lot like the pause on OpenAI that I have been recommending here for weeks. And I very much agree with the Florida AG that OpenAI doesn’t have the ability to properly regulate its own tech. Every state and country should follow Florida’s lead. In short, Florida gets it. We need an injunction NOW. Otherwise we are playing Russian roulette, hoping that some new software package can save us before some massive and possibly deadly AI-induced infrastructure problem strikes. Subscribe now P.S. Bonus analogy, riffing on a suggestion from Geoffrey Miller (and a similar suggestion earlier from Kathryn Hulick ) Share",
